              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Nostradamus</strong><br>\nMichel de Nostredame, commonly known as Nostradamus, was born on December 14, 1503, in Saint-Remy-de-Provence, France. He was one of nine children to a grain dealer and his wife. Nostradamus was educated by his grandfathers, who were physicians, and he later attended the University of Avignon to study medicine. Unfortunately, his studies were cut short when the university closed due to an outbreak of the plague.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Fight Against the Plague</strong><br>\nAfter leaving Avignon, Nostradamus began working as an apothecary, a kind of pharmacist, which was against university regulations. He eventually decided to pursue further education in medicine and enrolled at the University of Montpellier. There, he gained knowledge that would later help him fight the plague, which was ravaging Europe at the time. Despite the seriousness of the plague, his unconventional methods, like promoting cleanliness and the removal of infected corpses, helped save many lives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Marriage and Personal Loss</strong><br>\nNostradamus married and had two children in the early 1530s. Tragically, his wife and children died during another plague outbreak, a loss that greatly affected him. His in-laws sued for the return of the dowry, which added to his difficulties. This period of hardship influenced Nostradamus's later turn toward the occult and astrology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
