            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Shift to Astrology and Prophecy</strong><br>\nBy the 1540s, Nostradamus had begun moving away from medicine and toward the realms of astrology and prophecy. He started advising clients based on their astrological signs and produced horoscopes for them. His fame grew as he worked on an almanac that predicted the weather and other events. People began to regard him as a seer who could predict the future.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The 'Prophecies' and Its Impact</strong><br>\nIn 1555, Nostradamus published 'Les Prophéties,' a collection of 942 poetic quatrains believed to predict future events. The book became a success and brought Nostradamus fame throughout Europe. His predictions were seen by many as remarkably accurate, with some believing they foretold events such as the rise of Adolf Hitler and the French Revolution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Royal Attention and Endorsement</strong><br>\nNostradamus's growing reputation eventually attracted the attention of the French court, and he was appointed as the physician and counselor to King Henry II's wife, Catherine de Medici. She was fascinated by his predictions and supported his work. His association with the court elevated his status and he became a celebrated figure in France.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
