          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Continued Work</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Nostradamus continued to write prophecies and provide counsel based on astrology. He also published yearly almanacs that were widely popular, providing predictions and advice for agriculture, weather, and politics. Despite suffering from severe gout and arthritis, he remained dedicated to his work until his death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nNostradamus passed away on July 2, 1566, in Salon-de-Provence, France. After his death, his prophecies and writings continued to be popular, with many people interpreting them as they related to current events. His legacy has endured through the centuries, with a significant following that still studies his quatrains today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticism and Skepticism</strong><br>\nWhile Nostradamus has many supporters, his work has also faced criticism and skepticism. Some scholars argue that his quatrains are vague and can be interpreted in many ways, allowing people to apply them to various historical events after the fact. This debate over the validity of his predictions continues, with Nostradamus remaining a controversial figure in the history of prophecy.</p>\n"
              },
